/**
 * The Rustrak wordmark: the word `rustrak` set in Outfit 700 and converted to
 * outlines. There is no symbol, no monogram and no icon -- this single drawing
 * is the whole mark. Source of truth: `rustrak-brand`,
 * `brand/assets/logo/wordmark.svg`, generated by `tools/build_logo.py`.
 *
 * Two rules come with it, and both are the reason this file exists at all:
 *
 * 1. **The word is never typed.** Typing it makes the mark depend on which font
 *    the browser resolved, at which weight, and on the network not failing. The
 *    SVG is placed instead, so it is the same drawing every time. The only place
 *    `rustrak` is written as text is inside a prose sentence, where it is a word
 *    and not the mark.
 * 2. **It never goes in a box.** No tile, no rounded square, no field behind it
 *    at UI sizes.
 *
 * ## Sizing, and why it always looks too big at first
 *
 * The artwork box is trimmed to the ink -- no ascender room, no descender room.
 * So an 18px wordmark has 18px letters, while 18px *text* beside it has letters
 * of about 13px. Matched by box height the mark always over-powers its
 * neighbours; it wants to be set against the surrounding **cap height**, which
 * in practice means going one step smaller than feels right. The sizes in use
 * are 18px in the header, 22px on brand panels, 26px on the About card.
 *
 * Brand floors: 20px in the product header, 24-32px in docs, 14px absolute.
 * Below 14px the word stops being readable and the answer is the square poster
 * asset, not a smaller wordmark -- which is why a 1:1 slot (favicon, avatar, app
 * icon) does not use this component.
 *
 * ## Clear space belongs to the layout, not to the mark
 *
 * The brand asks for half an x-height on all four sides. That used to live in a
 * `RustrakWordmarkLockup` wrapper which applied it as padding -- and it was
 * wrong, because every call site puts the mark inside a link. Padding on a
 * wrapper inside the link is clickable but is not the `group`, so the ring
 * around the artwork took clicks while doing nothing on hover: a dead border you
 * could press.
 *
 * So the clear space is the container's job. Every surface that carries the mark
 * already gives it far more than the ratio asks for (`px-4` in the header,
 * `p-12` on the brand panels), and the interactive element now measures exactly
 * the artwork, so what you can click and what reacts are the same rectangle.
 *
 * ## Hover and press
 *
 * Both are borrowed from `Button` rather than designed here, so the mark answers
 * the pointer in the same language as every control around it:
 *
 * | | `Button` | here |
 * |---|---|---|
 * | Hover | colour only, no movement | lime wipes in, no movement |
 * | Press | `active:translate-y-px`, 1px down | the same, 1px down |
 * | Timing | bare `transition-all`: 150ms, `cubic-bezier(.4,0,.2,1)` | the same defaults |
 *
 * The wipe runs left to right with a hard leading edge, so the mark reads as
 * being converted rather than recoloured.
 *
 * An earlier version of this ran a bespoke pair of asymmetric curves -- 190ms
 * decelerating in, 130ms accelerating out -- which is textbook micro-interaction
 * practice and was still wrong here. Consistency of timing and easing across a
 * UI matters as much as consistency of colour, and a mark keeping private time
 * next to controls that all share one clock reads as a foreign element no matter
 * how well tuned it is on its own.
 *
 * The wipe is a CSS `clip-path` inset rather than an SVG `<clipPath>`, which
 * needs a unique id per instance and therefore a hook and a client boundary.
 * With the inset there is nothing to name, so this stays a server component with
 * no runtime attached.
 */

